    "created_at": "2018-07-24T18:33:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-07-24T18:33:43Z",
    "body": "A namespace is just a way of making tagnames or attribute names much longer, so you can mix markup languages together that weren't designed to be mixed, without any risk of the two languages accidentally having the same name for an element.  They have no other effect.\r\n\r\nThey are a legacy feature in HTML, as HTML is not designed to be mixed with arbitrary other markup languages. It can be mixed with SVG and MathML, that's all, and both of those either avoid name collisions in the short versions of their tagnames, or have specially-defined handling for when the short versions *do* collide (like `<html:script>` vs `<svg:script>`). If we ever allow more languages, they'll be designed similarly to avoid or automatically resolve collisions without people having to worry about namespaces.\r\n\r\nThe fact that namespaces look like URLs has no relevance to anything; the URL is meaningless in this context.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
