    "created_at": "2018-07-30T18:24:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-07-30T18:24:22Z",
    "body": "> And how does the browser understand what namespace it works with if there are 2 of them at once: html and svg?\r\n\r\nThere's nothing for browsers to \"work with\" for namespaces. It's just a way of writing a really long tagname.  HTML's script element's tagname is technically `(\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\", \"script\")` - a pair of strings - while SVG's script element's tagname is `(\"https://www.w3.org/2000/svg\", \"script\")` - a different pair of strings. This way, the browser can tell which element is which.\r\n\r\n> And please clarify the situation with (`<html:script>` and `<svg:script>`) - is it somehow possible to use it in your html code? I did not find such syntax in html.\r\n\r\nNo, that's just how you'd write the elements in XHTML (assuming you'd previously written `xmlns:html=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"` and `xmlns:svg=\"https://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"` attributes into an ancestor element).\r\n\r\nYou can't write that in HTML because, as I and others have stated, namespaces are a legacy feature in HTML and there is no reason to worry about them.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
